            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For indoor use, the Comfort Glow KW-24J is the best kerosene heater for most homes. Its listing gives 23,800 BTU for up to 1,000 sq ft, a 1.9-gallon tank that runs up to 12 hours, a fuel gauge and a tip-over shut-off. For one small room, the Sengoku HeatMate 10,000 BTU is the compact choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to use a kerosene heater indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can be, if your local law allows it and you follow the rules. Use a wick heater sold for indoor use, crack a window, keep a working battery CO detector nearby, keep 3 feet of clearance, use 1-K kerosene only and never run it while you sleep. Follow the maker's instructions for your model."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a kerosene heater give off carbon monoxide?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Every kerosene heater burns fuel and releases carbon monoxide and other combustion gases into the room. A well-tuned heater burning fresh 1-K fuel produces less, but never zero. That is why ventilation and a working CO detector are required, not optional, whenever you run one indoors. Headache, dizziness or nausea are warning signs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use a forced-air kerosene heater indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not in a living space. Forced-air or torpedo heaters are built for well-ventilated work areas such as construction sites, barns and workshops, and listings like Mr. Heater's say so. They blow exhaust directly into the space, need a power outlet and are loud. Use a convection or radiant wick heater for rooms in a home."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What kerosene should I use in a kerosene heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use 1-K (K-1) kerosene, the low-sulfur grade wick heaters are designed for. Buy it fresh from a trusted supplier, store it in its own labelled container, and never use gasoline, diesel, camp fuel or thinner in a wick heater. Stale or contaminated fuel causes most smoke and odor problems and can clog the wick early."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you replace a kerosene heater wick?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "There is no fixed schedule. Replace the wick when a dry burn, if your manual allows one, no longer restores a clean flame, or when it is hard, crusted, frayed or soaked with water or the wrong fuel. Many owners keep a spare matched to their heater's model number before winter starts."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I stop my kerosene heater from smelling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Use fresh 1-K kerosene, set the wick to the height the manual describes, and keep the wick clean. Most odor comes at lighting and shutdown, so some owners light and extinguish near an open door if their manual allows it. A strong, growing smell or smoke means turn it off and ventilate."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
