        <div id="indoor-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Can You Use a Kerosene Heater Indoors? Yes, With Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best kerosene heater for indoor use is a wick heater, either a round convection model or a reflector-style radiant model, with a tip-over shut-off and a battery or push-button igniter. These are the heaters sold for indoor use, and they are what most people mean when they search for one. They are popular for a simple reason: they need no electricity, so they keep a room warm when the power is out and the furnace blower is dead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor use comes with conditions, though, and they are not optional. A kerosene heater burns fuel inside your home and releases its combustion gases, including carbon monoxide, into the room. Used carelessly, it can poison or burn the people it is meant to keep warm. The rules below are what make indoor use reasonable.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check Your Local Law First</h3>

          <p>
            Some cities, counties and states restrict or ban unvented kerosene heaters in homes, and some landlords and insurers prohibit them. The rules vary widely, so check with your local fire department or building department before you buy, not after. If indoor use is not allowed where you live, look at the alternatives in our <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">indoor heaters for power outages</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wick Heaters Are for Rooms; Forced-Air Heaters Are Not</h3>

          <p>
            Forced-air kerosene heaters, the torpedo-shaped ones, are built for ventilated work areas such as construction sites, barns and workshops. Mr. Heater says exactly that in its listing. They blow exhaust straight into the space and are not meant for living areas or bedrooms. We include two because people search for them, but they are not indoor home heaters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Follow the Maker's Instructions</h3>

          <p>
            Every kerosene heater comes with a manual that covers fuel, clearance, ventilation, lighting and wick care for that exact model. Read it before the first lighting, and keep it with the heater. Where this guide and the manual differ, the manual and your local code win.
          </p>
        </div>

