          </h2>

          <p>
            Kerosene heaters come in three designs. The first two are wick heaters sold for indoor use; the third is a work-area heater. Choosing the right type matters more than choosing the brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Convection Heaters: Best for Large Rooms</h3>

          <p>
            Round convection heaters, like the Comfort Glow KW-24J, Dyna-Glo RMC-95C6B and Sengoku KeroHeat, sit in the middle of a room and push warm air up and out in every direction. They are usually rated between about 20,000 and 24,000 BTU, enough for a living room or open-plan ground floor. They are the best choice when the whole family gathers in one big space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radiant Heaters: Best for One Small Room</h3>

          <p>
            Reflector or radiant heaters, like the Sengoku HeatMate and Kero World 10,000 BTU, have a burner in front of a reflector that throws heat forward. They are usually about 10,000 BTU, warm the people in front of them quickly and suit a bedroom-sized room, office or den.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Forced-Air Heaters: For Ventilated Work Areas Only</h3>

