        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Kerosene Heater Safety: The Rules That Prevent Fires and CO Poisoning
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kerosene heaters are safe enough to use indoors only when you follow every one of these rules. None of them is a nice-to-have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilate: Crack a Window</h3>

          <p>
            An unvented heater uses oxygen from the room and releases combustion gases into it. Open a window an inch or open a door to a larger part of the house whenever the heater is running. A tightly sealed room is where carbon monoxide and low oxygen become dangerous.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Run a Working CO Detector</h3>

          <p>
            Put a battery-powered carbon monoxide detector in the room with the heater and one near the bedrooms. A plug-in alarm with no battery backup is useless in an outage. Test it before the season starts. Our <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">carbon monoxide detector guide</a> covers the alarms worth buying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Keep 3 Feet of Clearance and Use the Tip-Over Shut-Off</h3>

          <p>
            Keep at least 3 feet between the heater and furniture, curtains, bedding, paper and walls, or more if the manual says so. Set it on a flat, hard floor away from doorways and foot traffic. Buy a heater with a tip-over shut-off, and test it as the manual describes. Keep children and pets away from the hot grill.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Run It While Sleeping or Away</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the heater off before you go to bed and before you leave the house. Most kerosene heater fires and CO poisonings happen when nobody is awake to notice a problem. If the room needs to stay warm overnight, warm it before bed and use extra blankets.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Use 1-K Kerosene Only, and Never Gasoline
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fuel you put in a kerosene heater decides how cleanly it burns, how much it smells and whether it is safe at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What 1-K Kerosene Means</h3>

          <p>
            1-K (also written K-1) is the low-sulfur grade of kerosene that wick heaters are designed to burn. Grade 2-K has more sulfur, produces more odor and fumes, and can foul the wick faster. Buy 1-K from a dealer you trust, in clear or dyed form as your heater's manual allows.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Use Gasoline or Other Fuels</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline in a kerosene heater can cause a fire or explosion. Never use it, even a small amount mixed in. Do not use diesel, camp fuel, paint thinner or charcoal lighter fluid in a wick heater either. Some forced-air heaters accept other fuels, such as diesel, but only if their manual lists them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refuel Outdoors, When the Heater Is Cool</h3>

          <p>
            Turn the heater off, let it cool, and carry it or its removable tank outside to refill. Never fill a hot or running heater, and never fill it indoors where a spill soaks into the floor. Do not overfill; stop at the full mark on the gauge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Store Kerosene Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Keep kerosene in a container clearly labelled for it, usually blue, never in a red gasoline can, so nobody mixes them up. Store it outside the living area, away from heat. Kerosene left over from last season can pick up water and go stale; old fuel burns poorly and smells more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Kerosene Heater Do You Need? BTU by Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            Kerosene heaters are rated in BTU, and listings usually give a heated area as well. Treat those areas as best-case figures; a drafty room, high ceilings or bitter cold all reduce them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Up to About 400 Square Feet: 10,000 BTU</h3>

          <p>
            A 10,000 BTU radiant heater, like the Sengoku HeatMate or Kero World, suits a bedroom-sized room, office or small cabin. It uses less fuel and gives the room less to ventilate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Living Rooms and Open Plans: 23,000 to 24,000 BTU</h3>

          <p>
            A large convection heater, like the Comfort Glow KW-24J or Dyna-Glo RMC-95C6B, suits a big living room or the open ground floor of a house. More output means more combustion gas, so ventilation matters even more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do Kerosene Heaters Have Thermostats?</h3>

          <p>
            Most indoor wick heaters do not. You control output with the flame or wick adjuster, which is why a heater sized to your room is better than a bigger one turned low. Forced-air heaters, like the Mr. Heater and Dyna-Glo 80,000 BTU models, commonly do have thermostats. If you need precise thermostat control in a living space, an <a href="/best-electric-space-heater/">electric space heater</a> is the better tool when power is on.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wicks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Kerosene Heater Wicks: Burning Off and Replacement
          </h2>

          <p>
            The wick is the part of a kerosene heater that wears out, and how you treat it decides how well the heater burns. A healthy wick gives a clean, even flame; a clogged one smokes, smells and loses heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Burning Off a Wick (Dry Burning)</h3>

          <p>
            Tar and carbon build up on a wick over time, especially with old or contaminated fuel. Many fiberglass-wick heaters are cleaned by a dry burn: with the tank run empty, the heater burns until the flame goes out, which burns off deposits. Do it outdoors or in a well-ventilated space, and only if your manual recommends it, since some wicks are not designed for it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Replace a Wick</h3>

          <p>
            Replace the wick when a dry burn no longer brings back a clean flame, when it is hard, crusted or frayed, when the flame stays low or uneven, or when it got soaked with water or the wrong fuel. Many owners keep a spare wick with the heater before winter starts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy the Wick That Matches Your Model</h3>

          <p>
            Wicks are model-specific. Use your heater's model number and the maker's wick chart, and check the replacement's fit list carefully before you order. A wick that does not fit properly can cause a poor flame, smoke and odor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="odor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Why Kerosene Heaters Smell, and How to Reduce It
          </h2>

          <p>
            A properly running kerosene heater burning fresh 1-K fuel has a faint odor at most. A strong smell is a warning sign worth chasing down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lighting and Shutting Down Cause Most of It</h3>

          <p>
            Most odor comes in the minutes after lighting and when the flame goes out. Some owners light and extinguish the heater outdoors or near an open door, then bring it in once it is burning steadily, if the manual allows it. Let it burn at full height for the first few minutes rather than turning it down immediately.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bad Fuel and a Wrong Wick Height</h3>

          <p>
            Old, contaminated or high-sulfur fuel is the most common cause of a persistent smell. So is a wick set too high, which smokes, or too low, which burns incompletely. Drain stale fuel, refill with fresh 1-K, and adjust the flame as the manual describes.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Odor Means Stop</h3>

          <p>
            If you see smoke, the flame turns tall and yellow, the smell keeps getting stronger, or anyone gets a headache, dizziness or nausea, turn the heater off, ventilate the room and go outside. Those can be signs of carbon monoxide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-outage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Kerosene Heaters for Power Outages: Planning Fuel and Runtime
          </h2>

          <p>
            The main reason people buy a kerosene heater for indoor use is a winter power outage. Wick heaters need no electricity, so they keep working when the grid does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan Fuel by Runtime</h3>

          <p>
            Listings give runtime per tank: up to 12 hours for the large Comfort Glow and Sengoku convection heaters, and up to 14 hours for the Sengoku HeatMate. Since you should run the heater only while you are awake, one tank can last most of a day. Store enough fresh 1-K for the outages you usually see, and refill outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Kerosene vs Propane, Battery and Generator Heat</h3>

          <p>
            Propane heaters such as the Mr. Heater Buddy are another non-electric option; we compare them in our <a href="/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/">indoor heater for power outage</a> guide, and our <a href="/best-indoor-propane-heater/">indoor propane heater</a> rankings check each model's ODS sensor and BTU range. A <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for power outages</a> can run your furnace blower or an electric heater, and keeps every combustion appliance outdoors. Battery options are covered in our <a href="/best-battery-powered-heater/">battery-powered heater</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Bring Outdoor Heaters Inside</h3>

          <p>
            In an outage, do not bring a forced-air heater, a patio heater, a grill or a camp stove indoors for warmth. They are not designed for living spaces and can fill a house with carbon monoxide quickly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="garage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Best Kerosene Heater for a Garage or Workshop
          </h2>

          <p>
            Garages and workshops are where kerosene heaters earn their keep, but the right type depends on the space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ventilated Workshops and Barns: Forced Air</h3>

          <p>
            A forced-air heater such as the Mr. Heater or Dyna-Glo 80,000 BTU heats a big, drafty space fast. Keep a door or vent open, keep it clear of fuel and solvents, and keep a CO detector in the space. Never use one in an attached garage with the door closed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Small Garages and Benches: A Wick Heater</h3>

          <p>
            For a one- or two-car garage where you work at a bench, a 10,000 BTU radiant heater aimed at you is often enough, and quieter. Keep it away from gasoline, paint and anything flammable, since fuel vapors sink along the floor.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Consider Other Garage Heaters</h3>

          <p>
            If you heat a garage regularly, a hardwired electric or vented gas heater may suit you better. Our <a href="/best-garage-heater/">garage heater guide</a> compares them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Forced-Air Heater in a Living Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Torpedo heaters blow exhaust straight into the room and are made for ventilated work areas. Use a wick heater sold for indoor use, and only where local rules allow it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling It With the Wrong Fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline can cause a fire or explosion, and 2-K or stale kerosene smokes and smells. Use fresh 1-K kerosene only, stored in its own labelled container.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refueling Indoors or While It Is Hot</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spills inside soak into floors and create fumes, and a hot heater can ignite vapor. Turn it off, let it cool, and refill outdoors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running It Overnight in a Closed Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sleeping beside a running heater in a sealed room is how CO poisonings happen. Turn it off before bed, crack a window while it runs, and keep a battery CO alarm nearby.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a Dirty or Wrong Wick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A clogged or poorly fitting wick smokes, smells and loses heat. Dry burn it if the manual says so, and replace it with the exact part for your model.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

