        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best kerosene heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For indoor use, the Comfort Glow KW-24J is the best kerosene heater for most homes. Its listing gives 23,800 BTU for up to 1,000 sq ft, a 1.9-gallon tank that runs up to 12 hours, a fuel gauge and a tip-over shut-off. For one small room, the Sengoku HeatMate 10,000 BTU is the compact choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a kerosene heater indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be, if your local law allows it and you follow the rules. Use a wick heater sold for indoor use, crack a window, keep a working battery CO detector nearby, keep 3 feet of clearance, use 1-K kerosene only and never run it while you sleep. Follow the maker's instructions for your model.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a kerosene heater give off carbon monoxide?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Every kerosene heater burns fuel and releases carbon monoxide and other combustion gases into the room. A well-tuned heater burning fresh 1-K fuel produces less, but never zero. That is why ventilation and a working CO detector are required, not optional, whenever you run one indoors. Headache, dizziness or nausea are warning signs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a forced-air kerosene heater indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in a living space. Forced-air or torpedo heaters are built for well-ventilated work areas such as construction sites, barns and workshops, and listings like Mr. Heater's say so. They blow exhaust directly into the space, need a power outlet and are loud. Use a convection or radiant wick heater for rooms in a home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kerosene should I use in a kerosene heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use 1-K (K-1) kerosene, the low-sulfur grade wick heaters are designed for. Buy it fresh from a trusted supplier, store it in its own labelled container, and never use gasoline, diesel, camp fuel or thinner in a wick heater. Stale or contaminated fuel causes most smoke and odor problems and can clog the wick early.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you replace a kerosene heater wick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed schedule. Replace the wick when a dry burn, if your manual allows one, no longer restores a clean flame, or when it is hard, crusted, frayed or soaked with water or the wrong fuel. Many owners keep a spare matched to their heater's model number before winter starts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop my kerosene heater from smelling?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use fresh 1-K kerosene, set the wick to the height the manual describes, and keep the wick clean. Most odor comes at lighting and shutdown, so some owners light and extinguish near an open door if their manual allows it. A strong, growing smell or smoke means turn it off and ventilate.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

